Minimal, localized fix in src/hud.js projectMeter (line ~359): refresh the camera's world matrix (which in r186 also recomputes matrixWorldInverse via the Camera override, with no allocation) before projecting:

  const v = SCRATCH.v3a.set(x, y, z);
  if (cam.matrixWorldAutoUpdate !== false && cam.parent === null) cam.updateMatrixWorld();
  v.project(cam);

This only runs while the meter is visible (windup/layup), so it adds one matrix compose + 4x4 inverse per frame only during a windup, and renderer.render's own later update is then a cheap no-op-equivalent. The alternative from the finding (calling c.updateMatrixWorld() at the end of BroadcastCamera.apply()) is also correct and allocation-free (spec section 9 requires no per-frame allocations) but does the work every frame; prefer the hud-local version. Verify with the repro script: after the fix the meter transform must equal the projection composed from cam.position/quaternion on the same frame (errPx ~0).

Minimal, spec-consistent fix in src/hud.js projectMeter(), immediately before line 359:

    if (cam.parent === null) cam.updateMatrixWorld(true);
    const v = SCRATCH.v3a.set(x, y, z).project(cam);

Camera.updateMatrixWorld recomputes matrix from the position/rotation apply() just set, copies it to matrixWorld and refreshes matrixWorldInverse, so the projection uses this frame's pose. Cost: one 4x4 invert per frame only while the meter is active (renderer.render will redo it, which is harmless). Guarding on parent===null keeps the call correct for the current unparented broadcast camera; if a parented camera is ever passed, use cam.updateWorldMatrix(true, false) instead. Doing it in BroadcastCamera.apply() (as the finding proposes) also works but pays the invert every frame, not just during windups, and is not needed since nothing else reads the camera's world matrices between apply() and renderer.render().

